>So, I'm only on dungeon level 3, and I find an armor shop.  And, much
>to my euphoria, I've found gauntlets of power, speed boots, and two
>cloaks:  a cloak of protection, and a cloak of displacement.  Question
>is, which should I be wearing?  I'm a level 4 priest, and without
>either my AC is 5.  Basically, which is going to keep me alive longer?

  It depends on how effectively you use them.

  Displacement causes monsters to initially misjudge your location by up
to two squares.  This has a lot of entertainment potential.

  They'll keep trying new squares until they find you though.  And once
they do find you they'll just keep hitting you.

  The secret to effective use of displacement is to move once the
monsters start hitting you, because that means they've found you.
(Krysia's Krusader referred to this movement as "reactivating" the
cloak.)  This is actually kind of interesting.

  One downside of displacement is Elbereth becomes less effective.  Your
displaced image may not appear to be on an Elbereth.  The trick is to
drop an item on the Elbereth square to keep it active.

  One thing to keep in mind is that you can still change cloaks
according to the situation.  This mostly applies to the robe for spell
casting.  Even the most power-mad spell caster (i.e. me) spends less
than 1% of the time casting spells.  

  Not all of those spells will be cast in combat.  Attack is just one of
many spell schools.  When not in combat it doesn't hurt anything to take
off the displacement and wear the robe to help spell casting.  (This
technique of optimizing armor for spell casting also applies to shields,
and even body armor (with extra care).)

  Another aspect is that the cloak of protection can lose its advantages
via rotting/burning.  The displacement cloak won't.


  So play around with displacement.  See what you think.  Some players
really like it once they realize how to take full advantage of it.  It's
really fun to watch a monster throw missiles in the exact opposite
direction from you, for example.



